\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Even volume exchanges opposite symbol eigenspaces}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{In even dimension #{n=2m\ge2} let the self-adjoint direction
operators on a nonzero complex Hermitian Clifford module satisfy
the positive Euclidean relations. Use the volume grading
#{C=\ui^m\psI} of \ref{fdip-009X} and a Hermitian symbol
#{-\ui\mathcal D(p)=\sum_j f_j(r)\varepsilon_j} with real
coefficients. The operator #{C} bijects the eigenspace at
#{\xi} with that at #{-\xi}. If #{\xi\ne0}, neither eigenspace
contains a nonzero joint eigenvector of the symbol and #{C}.}

\proof{
\p{The ordered product anticommutes with each direction because
conjugating by one generator negates the other #{n-1}
factors. Thus #{C} anticommutes with the whole sum.
If the symbol takes #{v} to #{\xi v}, it takes #{C v} to
#{-\xi C v}. Since #{C^2=\bbI_S} by \ref{fdip-009X}, the same
map gives the inverse from the opposite eigenspace. When
#{\xi\ne0}, the real numbers #{\xi} and #{-\xi} are distinct.
If #{C v=\pm v} for a vector in one of these spaces, it
also belongs to the other. Their intersection is zero.
At symbol zero the entire zero eigenspace is preserved.
The sign of a nonzero symbol eigenvalue used in
\ref{fdip-017B} is unavailable there.}
}

\p{TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{Module.End.map_genEigenspace_of_anticommute}
transports generalized eigenspaces by an invertible anticommuting
map. The argument above applies directly to ordinary eigenspaces.}
